itive cone $P$, and","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L4","source":"lem-finite-sum-laws","source_section":"Statement","quote":"$\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and let $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, with finite sums and","uses":["2.2"]},{"fact":"L6","source":"thm-mertens","source_section":"Statement","quote":"2. **Mertens' theorem.** If $\\sum a_k$ converges absolutely","uses":["3.1","4.1"]},{"fact":"L7","source":"def-real-limit","source_section":"Definition","quote":"A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals **converges** to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ when for","uses":["2.1"]}],"derivations":[{"id":"step-1-1","claim":"The series $\\sum_k a_k$ converges, and its Cauchy product with itself satisfies $|c_n| = \\sum_{k=0}^{n}\\beta_k\\beta_{n-k} \\ge 2\\iota(n+1)/\\iota(n+2) \\ge 1$ for every $n$.","step":"1.1","inputs":["L1"]},{"id":"step-2-1","claim":"Hence $(c_n)$ does not converge to $0$: the tolerance $1$ admits no index $K$ with $|c_n - 0| < 1$ for all $n \\ge K$. So $\\sum c_n$ diverges, and two convergent series can have a divergent Cauchy product.","step":"2.1","inputs":["1.1","L1","L7"]},{"id":"step-2-2","claim":"The lower bound is itself informative: $2\\iota(n+1)/\\iota(n+2) = 2 - 2/\\iota(n+2)$, a quantity strictly increasing in $n$ that exceeds every real below $2$ from some index on. So $|c_n| \\ge 2 - 2/\\iota(n+2)$ for every $n$, and the terms of the product series stay bounded away from $0$ by an amount approaching $2$; nothing here claims a value for $|c_n|$ itself, only this bound for it.","step":"2.2","inputs":["1.1","L3","L4"]},{"id":"step-3-1","claim":"Neither factor converges absolutely, and that is exactly what the hypothesis of Mertens' theorem asks for: were $\\sum |a_k|$ convergent, [L6] would make $\\sum c_n$ convergent, contradicting step 2.1.","step":"3.1","inputs":["2.1","L6"]},{"id":"step-4-1","claim":"So the refuted claim fails for this pair, and the hypothesis that repairs it is absolute convergence of one factor.","step":"4.1","inputs":["2.1","2.2","3.1","L6"]}],"routine